  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "When \\(p = \\sum_{k=1}^{6} k \\ln k,\\) the number \\(e^p\\) is an integer. What is the largest power of \\(2\\) that is a factor of \\(e^p?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(2^{12}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(2^{14}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(2^{16}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(2^{18}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(2^{20}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12b-2014-p15.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(k \\ln k = \\ln(k^k),\\) the sum gives \\(p = \\ln\\left(\\prod_{k=1}^{6} k^k\\right),\\) so \\[ e^p = 1^1 \\cdot 2^2 \\cdot 3^3 \\cdot 4^4 \\cdot 5^5 \\cdot 6^6. \\]\n\nThe factors of \\(2\\) come from \\(2^2\\) (giving \\(2\\)), \\(4^4 = 2^8\\) (giving \\(8\\)), and \\(6^6 = 2^6 \\cdot 3^6\\) (giving \\(6\\)). In total the exponent of \\(2\\) is \\(2 + 8 + 6 = 16.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "\\(k \\ln k = \\ln(k^k),\\) so \\(e^p = \\prod_{k=1}^{6} k^k\\)",
        "Only \\(2^2,\\) \\(4^4,\\) and \\(6^6\\) contribute factors of \\(2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
